---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Diagram from '../../components/Diagram.astro';
import DBox from '../../components/DBox.astro';
import { facts } from '../../data/facts';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/+$/, '');
---

<Base
  title="A convention copied 1,600 times — Andrew Yang"
  description="A Gradle convention plugin centralised how every book repository builds. What it did not centralise showed up years later as 36 GB of duplicated build artifacts and a fleet split across four plugin versions."
>
  <p><a href={`${base}/lab/`}>← Lab</a></p>
  <span class="tag">fleet management</span>
  <h1>A convention copied 1,600 times</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A <a href={`${base}/platform/`}>convention plugin</a> centralised how every
    repository in the fleet builds. Two things it did not centralise stayed
    invisible until the fleet was large enough to measure them: 36 GB of
    duplicated build artifacts, and a fleet quietly split across four versions of
    the plugin that was supposed to be keeping it uniform. Both had the same
    root cause, and the same fix — stop distributing by value.
  </p>

  <Diagram
    viewBox="0 0 920 140"
    alt="Three generations left to right: the convention copied into every build file, then the convention published once as a plugin that each repository pins by version, then the convention referenced as a range with tool caches keyed by version instead of by repository. The first arrow is labelled with a rule change costing a hundred edits; the second with 36 GB of duplicated caches and a fleet split across four live plugin versions."
    caption="Each generation moved one more thing out of the repository. What stayed behind is what broke next — generation 2 centralised the build logic and left both the tool caches and the version string copied 1,600 times."
  >
    <DBox x={10} y={36} w={230} h={64} title="Gen 1 — copied per repo" sub="every build file spells out the" sub2="versions; uniform only after a sweep" />
    <DBox x={335} y={36} w={230} h={64} title="Gen 2 — a shared plugin" sub="build logic in one place — but the" sub2="version is still a pinned string" />
    <DBox x={660} y={36} w={230} h={64} accent title="Gen 3 — by reference" sub="caches keyed by version, not repo;" sub2="the version declared as a range" />
    <path class="flow" d="M240,68 L329,68" marker-end="url(#d-arrow)" />
    <text class="lbl" x="284" y="26" text-anchor="middle">one rule change</text>
    <text class="lbl" x="284" y="120" text-anchor="middle">= a hundred edits</text>
    <path class="flow" d="M565,68 L654,68" marker-end="url(#d-arrow)" />
    <text class="lbl" x="609" y="26" text-anchor="middle">36 GB duplicated,</text>
    <text class="lbl" x="609" y="120" text-anchor="middle">four live versions</text>
  </Diagram>

  <h2>The problem that does not change</h2>
  <p>
    One operator, {facts.repoCount}+ repositories, and every one of them has to
    build the same way: same Hugo version, same formatting rule, same task graph.
    The question is never <em>what</em> the convention is. It is where the
    convention <em>lives</em>, and what it costs to change it.
  </p>

  <h2>Generation 1 — the convention lives in every repository</h2>
  <p>
    Each repository carried its own build file with the versions written out.
    Fine at ten repositories. At a hundred, changing one formatting rule meant a
    hundred edits, and the fleet was only as uniform as the last sweep.
  </p>

  <h2>Generation 2 — the convention becomes a plugin</h2>
  <p>
    A Gradle convention plugin, published to a package registry, applied by every
    repository. The build logic moved to one place; each repository declared
    which version of it to apply, and an automated dependency bot opened the
    upgrade pull requests. This is the arrangement described on the
    <a href={`${base}/platform/`}>platform page</a>, and it held for a long time.
  </p>

  <h2>What forced generation 3</h2>
  <p>
    Two failures, both of which only become measurable at fleet scale.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The tool caches were never centralised.</strong> The plugin
    centralised the build <em>logic</em>, but the tools that logic drives — the
    Hugo binary, the formatter's npm dependencies — still downloaded into each
    repository's own build directory. A census across the fleet found 35.8 GB of
    build output, of which 24.6 GB was one formatter's <code>node_modules</code>,
    spread across 1,667 directories that held <strong>three</strong> distinct
    dependency sets between them. One of those three had been copied 1,538
    times. Alongside it, a 52 MB Hugo binary, downloaded and extracted once per
    repository. Against all of that, the actual writing — every chapter, every
    image, in every book — came to 3.5 GB.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The version was distributed by value.</strong> A pinned version is a
    string copied into every build file, and the dependency bot is the process
    that keeps 1,600 copies of that string equal. It did not. A census found the
    fleet split 888 / 391 / 287 / 1 across four versions, with 287 repositories
    still on a release from two minor versions back. Pinning was supposed to buy
    reproducibility. What it actually bought was drift with a changelog — worse
    than a floating version, because it looked deliberate.
  </p>

  <h2>Generation 3 — one cache, one declaration</h2>
  <ul class="clean">
    <li>
      <strong>Tool caches move out of the repository.</strong> Both downloads now
      resolve to a machine-level cache keyed by the pinned version. The formatter
      hardlinks each repository's copy into that cache rather than reinstalling,
      which drops the marginal cost of a repository from 16 MB to 560 KB; the
      Hugo download is skipped outright once the shared binary exists, so no
      repository past the first even fetches the tarball.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The version is declared once, by reference.</strong> Build files no
      longer name a version at all; the fleet's plugin version is declared in one
      block per repository as a range, so publishing the plugin <em>is</em> the
      rollout. The migration that moved 1,600 repositories to that form is
      designed to be the last bulk edit of its kind.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What I would tell my past self</h2>
  <p>
    Separate three layers and keep them separate. <strong>Machine-level:</strong>
    tool binaries and package caches, which depend on pinned versions and not on
    content. <strong>Fleet-level:</strong> the convention itself — one
    definition, referenced. <strong>Repository-level:</strong> the content, which
    is the only thing that is genuinely per-repository. Storage waste comes from
    binding the first layer to the third. Rollout pain comes from distributing
    the second by value instead of by reference. Both bills arrive late, in
    proportion to how well the thing worked.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <p><strong>Operational scar tissue from the migration</strong></p>
    <ul class="clean">
      <li>
        <strong>Verify before you clear.</strong> The migration re-declares the
        formatting step, which is only safe if every plugin version still in the
        fleet defines that step identically. Decompiling the oldest release in
        use to confirm took ten minutes, and was the difference between a safe
        migration and silently dropping a formatting rule from 287 repositories.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The sweep must be idempotent, because the fleet moves while you
        sweep it.</strong> Three passes turned up three different sets of
        stragglers. Not flaky tooling — new repositories were being provisioned
        during the migration. The script skips anything already migrated, which
        makes re-running it the entire recovery procedure.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Deploy-on-push is a throttle you own.</strong> Every repository
        publishes to Pages on push to its default branch, so a 1,600-repository
        migration commit either carries a skip-CI marker or becomes 1,600
        deploys.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The template is the fleet's germline.</strong> New repositories
        are instantiated from a template repository, so until the template is
        migrated too, every newly provisioned repository is born with the old
        form — and the sweep never converges.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</Base>
